{"step_index":58,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:55:42Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:55:42+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:55:47+02:00\nThe search for \"\"diam(G)\" \"gamma(G)\" domination number bound\" returned the following summary:\nThe relationship between the **diameter** ($\\text{diam}(G)$) and the **domination number** ($\\gamma(G)$) of a graph is a significant topic in domination theory. While there is no single simple formula that bounds $\\gamma(G)$ solely by $\\text{diam}(G)$ for all graphs, there are established upper bounds and specific behaviors for graphs with small diameters.\n\n### Key Insights and Bounds\n\n*   **Diameter 2 Graphs:**\n    *   For graphs with diameter 2, the domination number $\\gamma(G)$ can be constrained more tightly than in general graphs.\n    *   Research has shown that for a graph $G$ of diameter 2 and order $n$, the domination number is generally bounded by $\\gamma(G) \\le \\lfloor n/4 \\rfloor$ for sufficiently large $n$ (specifically $n \\ge 24$)[1].\n    *   Earlier results provided bounds of $O(n^{3/4})$, which have been improved over time to results such as $\\gamma(G) \\le (\\frac{1}{2} + o(1))\\sqrt{n \\log n}$ as $n \\to \\infty$[2].\n*   **Diameter 3 Graphs:**\n    *   In graphs with diameter 3, it has been shown that the domination number can be as large as $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$, but it does not exceed this value[2].\n*   **General Context:**\n    *   In the absence of diameter constraints, the most well-known general upper bound for a connected graph with no isolated vertices is $\\gamma(G) \\le n/2$[3][4][5].\n    *   The domination number is a fundamental graph invariant[6], and finding it is NP-complete for general graphs[3][7]. Consequently, researchers often look for bounds based on other parameters like maximum degree ($\\Delta$), radius, and diameter to provide more accurate estimates for specific graph classes[3][8].\n\n### Related Concepts\n*   **Vizing's Conjecture:** This famous conjecture relates the domination number of the Cartesian product of two graphs to the product of their individual domination numbers ($\\gamma(G \\Box H) \\ge \\gamma(G)\\gamma(H)$)[9][10][11]. While distinct from diameter-based bounds, it is a central theme in domination theory that often appears alongside research into diameter-constrained graphs[12][13].\n*   **Total Domination:** Similar studies exist for the total domination number ($\\gamma_t(G)$), which also rely on graph structure, degrees, and diameter[14][15].\n\nFor specific applications or if you are working with a particular class of graphs (e.g., planar, chordal, or regular), researchers often use more specialized bounds that incorporate the graph's structural properties, as simple diameter-based bounds can be quite loose for general graphs[16].\n\nSources:\n[1] [semanticscholar.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGVkogoiSRAtDVI-_aOpna6kQ3j9_a_vXxWuSHQpVntONec4CgOA-8VOV70eMfsiXD0Hy1G3SncfEvXDNiS1k8CPRA6fQinm04EDj2QawmuvGbcUTe-6RAQMIkzanXpUzwVy-NHdda5iqQagYwljSTUMCdxK3_otLhrcRAQYxfckeKSguEt0-pE1pjHs852CxpSy0r8F_1eC4ygCQvAL8Z_3S00MDf11hwSq7Xl2UDP10TfzFSHkz3-Pp3m8Ydv54noS5sgAx-fi2mdZ8Y=)\n[2] [combinatorialpress.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGrXq7CPjPf3BLvhYSqFfCeZNGvFEhc7KU8UDvu0CgQgpPEmlUVnh6XcWhf0W3AsKvCaWyJaGbd9PX9vtISZQhxQZ0hXVHB70TIxFYw3ez8L64E0OGO_v5t-LYZXJCQIZvPxP1fz5lGfLIhEBih1_10ZE7TQTwFbmvatgAuchUhDpacKy8ObJZ8J2Z89fL5WE2r6SqSMyQD9IhagkBA55-ANg==)\n[3] [wolfram.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGwUOMVV7PtOzc-Z1sQbRquCxvi98KPzOnp4Evk1Sd6dEvR0tPwQTOkJkg8ztL7R1bM3Wi5ZVWPTKCSI4Uy91WbKKZM12N7U6J3344S9IA8QHIbfKF9aoEtK5uH2-k-RGa_6mB-3TaxluA=)\n[4] [uz.zgora.pl](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHioeaduEjE5lL6qAYt-sitGESFHwR0GkkEybgzk8I0vBWzIPkfBB-FtL5BkfZcldICdLRG-e1N1_m6PmsIi2Ug_vnMZiaB0q1qYudR2V6kWY5_dmLzp5f