{"step_index":70,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:27Z","thinking":"**Exploring Graph Radius Limitations**\n\nThe maximum radius for k=2 is confirmed as 3, which respects the condition $3 + 1 \\le \\gamma_R$. Now considering k=3, the question is if the radius can be greater than 5. A hypothesis is that a graph with $\\gamma_R = 4$ cannot have a radius of 4; rather, the max radius is 3. Similarly, for $\\gamma_R = 5$, what is the maximum radius? Investigating further for the specific case of k=3 with domination number 3 and Roman domination number 6 is the next step.\n\n\n**Searching for radius constraints**\n\nThe analysis focuses on exploring if there exists any graph where radius plus one exceeds the Roman domination number. Cycles were examined initially, observing that the Roman domination number minus radius plus one is always non-negative. This is followed by a test on graphs within networkx generator (wheel, ladder, etc.) along with random graphs to find a possible counterexample to the established relationship.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import milp, LinearConstraint, Bounds\n\ndef roman_domination(G):\n    n = G.number_of_nodes()\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\n    node_idx = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(nodes)}\n    c = np.array([1]*n + [2]*n, dtype=float)\n    integrality = np.ones(2*n)\n    A_rows = []\n    lhs = []\n    rhs = []\n    for i, v in enumerate(nodes):\n        row1 = [0]*(2*n)\n        row1[i] = 1\n        row1[n + i] = 1\n        A_rows.append(row1)\n        lhs.append(0)\n        rhs.append(1)\n        \n        row2 = [0]*(2*n)\n        row2[i] = 1\n        row2[n + i] = 1\n        for u in G.neighbors(v):\n            row2[n + node_idx[u]] = 1\n        A_rows.append(row2)\n        lhs.append(1)\n        rhs.append(np.inf)\n        \n    constraints = LinearConstraint(A_rows, lhs, rhs)\n    bounds = Bounds(0, 1)\n    res = milp(c=c, integrality=integrality, constraints=constraints, bounds=bounds)\n    return int(round(res.fun))\n\ngraphs = []\nfor n in range(3, 16):\n    graphs.append(('ladder', nx.ladder_graph(n)))\n    graphs.append(('circular_ladder', nx.circular_ladder_graph(n)))\n    graphs.append(('wheel', nx.wheel_graph(n)))\nfor n in range(3, 10):\n    for k in range(1, n//2 + 1):\n        try:\n            graphs.append((f'gp_{n}_{k}', nx.generalized_petersen_graph(n, k)))\n        except:\n            pass\n\ncounterexamples = []\nfor name, G in graphs:\n    if nx.is_connected(G):\n        r = nx.radius(G)\n        rd = roman_domination(G)\n        diff = (r + 1) - rd\n        if diff > 0:\n            print(f'COUNTEREXAMPLE: {name}, n={G.number_of_nodes()}, r={r}, rd={rd}')\n            counterexamples.append((name, G))\n        else:\n            # print tight cases\n            if diff == 0:\n                print(f'TIGHT: {name}, n={G.number_of_nodes()}, r={r}, rd={rd}')\n\nprint(f'Done testing named graphs. Found {len(counterexamples)} counterexamples.')\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Running command","toolSummary":"Test named graphs in networkx"}}]}
