{"step_index":72,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:35Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import milp, LinearConstraint, Bounds\nimport random\n\ndef roman_domination(G):\n    n = G.number_of_nodes()\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\n    node_idx = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(nodes)}\n    c = np.array([1]*n + [2]*n, dtype=float)\n    integrality = np.ones(2*n)\n    A_rows = []\n    lhs = []\n    rhs = []\n    for i, v in enumerate(nodes):\n        row1 = [0]*(2*n)\n        row1[i] = 1\n        row1[n + i] = 1\n        A_rows.append(row1)\n        lhs.append(0)\n        rhs.append(1)\n        \n        row2 = [0]*(2*n)\n        row2[i] = 1\n        row2[n + i] = 1\n        for u in G.neighbors(v):\n            row2[n + node_idx[u]] = 1\n        A_rows.append(row2)\n        lhs.append(1)\n        rhs.append(np.inf)\n        \n    constraints = LinearConstraint(A_rows, lhs, rhs)\n    bounds = Bounds(0, 1)\n    res = milp(c=c, integrality=integrality, constraints=constraints, bounds=bounds)\n    return int(round(res.fun))\n\n# Local search to maximize r + 1 - rd\nfound = False\nfor n in [6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12]:\n    for restart in range(10):\n        # start with a random tree or path\n        G = nx.path_graph(n)\n        for step in range(50):\n            r = nx.radius(G)\n            rd = roman_domination(G)\n            diff = (r + 1) - rd\n            if diff > 0:\n                print(f'FOUND COUNTEREXAMPLE! n={n}, diff={diff}, edges={list(G.edges())}')\n                found = True\n                break\n            # mutate: add or remove an edge\n            u, v = random.sample(range(n), 2)\n            if G.has_edge(u, v):\n                if G.degree(u) > 1 and G.degree(v) > 1:\n                    G.remove_edge(u, v)\n                    if not nx.is_connected(G):\n                        G.add_edge(u, v)\n            else:\n                G.add_edge(u, v)\n        if found:\n            break\n    if found:\n        break\nif not found:\n    print('No counterexample found by local search!')\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","WaitMsBeforeAsync":15000,"toolAction":"Running command","toolSummary":"Run local search for counterexample"}}]}
{"step_index":73,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"RUNNING","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:39Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:56:39+02:00\nTool is running as a background task with task id: 92752dbc-e919-4b9e-b022-202726747cbe/task-73\nTask Description: /mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import milp, LinearConstraint, Bounds\nimport random\n\ndef roman_domination(G):\n    n = G.number_of_nodes()\n    nodes = list(G.nodes())\n    node_idx = {v: i for i, v in enumerate(nodes)}\n    c = np.array([1]*n + [2]*n, dtype=float)\n    integrality = np.ones(2*n)\n    A_rows = []\n    lhs = []\n    rhs = []\n    for i, v in enumerate(nodes):\n        row1 = [0]*(2*n)\n        row1[i] = 1\n        row1[n + i] = 1\n        A_rows.append(row1)\n        lhs.append(0)\n        rhs.append(1)\n        \n        row2 = [0]*(2*n)\n        row2[i] = 1\n        row2[n + i] = 1\n        for u in G.neighbors(v):\n            row2[n + node_idx[u]] = 1\n        A_rows.append(row2)\n        lhs.append(1)\n        rhs.append(np.inf)\n        \n    constraints = LinearConstraint(A_rows, lhs, rhs)\n    bounds = Bounds(0, 1)\n    res = milp(c=c, integrality=integrality, constraints=constraints, bounds=bounds)\n    return int(round(res.fun))\n\n# Local search to maximize r + 1 - rd\nfound = False\nfor n in [6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12]:\n    for restart in range(10):\n        # start with a random tree or path\n        G = nx.path_graph(n)\n        for step in range(50):\n            r = nx.radius(G)\n            rd = roman_domination(G)\n            diff = (r + 1) - rd\n            if diff > 0:\n                print(f'FOUND COUNTEREXAMPLE! n={n}, diff={diff}, edges={list(G.edges())}')\n                found = True\n                break\n            # mutate: add or remove an edge\n            u, v = random.sample(range(n), 2)\n            if G.has_edge(u, v):\n                if G.degree(u) > 1 and G.degree(v) > 1:\n                    G.remove_edge(u, v)\n                    if not nx.is_connected(G):\n                        G.add_edge(u, v)\n            else:\n                G.add_edge(u, v)\n        if found:\n            break\n    if found:\n        break\nif not found:\n    print('No counterexample found by local search!')\n\"\nTask logs are available at: file:///tmp/agyhome.NWyWnj/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/92752dbc-e919-4b9e-b022-202726747cbe/.system_generated/tasks/task-73.log\nYOU MUST TAKE ONE OF THE FOLLOWING TWO ACTIONS: A) either proceed to other relevant work (if any) or, B) simply update the user with a short message (that you have launched the command and will wait for it to finish) and end the turn.\n DO NOTHING ELSE."}
{"step_index":74,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:49Z","tool_calls":[{"name":"search_web","args":{"query":"\"roman domination number\" \"radius of a graph\"","toolAction":"Searching the web","toolSummary":"Search literature for radius and roman domination"}}]}
{"step_index":75,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T14:56:51Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T16:56:51+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T16:56:55+02:00\nThe search for \"\"roman domination number\" \"radius of a graph\"\" returned the following summary:\nThe **Roman domination number** ($\\gamma_R(G)$) and the **radius** of a graph ($\\text{rad}(G)$) are two distinct structural parameters in graph theory that are often studied together to understand the efficiency and reach of domination sets within a graph's topology[1][2][3].\n\n### Definitions\n*   **Roman Domination Number ($\\gamma_R(G)$):** This is the minimum weight of a Roman dominating function, which is a labeling $f: V \\to \\{0, 1, 2\\}$ such that every vertex labeled $0$ is adjacent to at least one vertex labeled $2$[4][5]. It models scenarios where resources (legions) must be positioned to cover all vertices, where a \"2\" represents a vertex with a legion capable of defending itself and its neighbors, and \"1\" represents a vertex with a legion that can defend itself[6][7].\n*   **Radius ($\\text{rad}(G)$):** The radius is defined as the minimum eccentricity among all vertices in the graph[4][8]. The eccentricity of a vertex $u$ is the maximum distance from $u$ to any other vertex in the graph[4][8].\n\n### Relationship and Research\nResearch often explores how these parameters interact to provide insight into graph structure[2]. Key points regarding their relationship include:\n\n*   **Comparative Studies:** There is academic interest in comparing the Roman domination number with the radius, as well as other parameters like the total domination number and distance-2 domination number[1][3]. These studies aim to establish bounds or correlations that hold for specific classes of graphs (e.g., trees, trapezoid graphs, or grid graphs)[1][9][3].\n*   **Structural Bounds:** Mathematicians often seek to establish bounds for domination parameters in terms of graph invariants like the radius, diameter, and maximum degree[10][11]. For instance, certain broadcast domination numbers have been shown to be determined by the minimum of the domination number and the radius[9][12].\n*   **Applications:** Because both the radius (often associated with center/location problems) and the Roman domination number (associated with resource allocation/defense) deal with vertex coverage and distance, they are frequently used together in operations research, communications network design, and facility location problems[1][13][14].\n\nIf you are looking for specific mathematical inequalities or proofs relating these two values for a particular class of graphs, it is often found in papers focusing on \"extremal graphs\" or \"bounds for domination numbers\"[15][16][11].\n\nSources:\n[1] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG7IYP3_qiu4voW_E3vZZB80F7mEeTZZ7CmVMerJYwQ71_tHqEqqUAFBRUkWzCVbnY0a1imnMv5xikVIDrVNpxyF55PD070jgWjt_e1nXHSGzSFdvM7_o3mw3zLsGrdqsemmKM=)\n[2] [worldofbooks.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEjjH-PHnNTTw3_GO77LagNbB2adClFId1wkTOPZ_1GP2IU4E6DnqwMRLy4uWKpzuN0Ilelwe6tUicquohd5jMsW3A5U7qAX5gfR6Azde6YzMoVxI9P0iHUaIu4t8rkOC-PC4-GxRwB4qjFDBH5mAn3iM6YduBhAeOCTS6jnrxfMunkWF-1Qj0PuiD_nH69fSgZsmfiZ6CttLKMqvWSYE0KQ29L8PDb)\n[3] [ijsdr.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG7IYP3_qiu4voW_E3vZZB80F7mEeTZZ7CmVMerJYwQ71_tHqEqqUAFBRUkWzCVbnY0a1imnMv5xikVIDrVNpxyF55PD070jgWjt_e1nXHSGzSFdvM7_o3mw3zLsGrdqsemmKM=)\n[4] [dergipark.org.tr](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFAJ9hWe2zwQDbxjmKaLz02esny0cMGF7tSpQaYnFdEKk6dQP9YsNW9e5W21VWfLEs6aJFpKsn_ZITJIiNpZejBBdrrKxb1L9u1NA6V5jFxAasSoTr5zZBWO7gW9iM01e9Uyind-JIUNngccH7Q76I=)\n[5] [mdpi.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGeZXBOCb5Nz8HgUcIh-N-JuVQcUfqKSLMB86GBoGXCf-KnmFSEW8IBu59QQ9jgdoPVxT7Y46LJ-Yr88gOq_7C2AGq1SgyBmVv30f